User: A performing magician has a disappearing act where he makes a random member of his audience disappear and reappear. Unfortunately, one-tenth of the time, the audience member never reappears. However, one-fifth of the time, two people reappear instead of only one. If the magician has put on 100 performances of the act this year, how many people have reappeared? Write your answer in \boxed{} format.

Assistant: Let's start by calculating the number of times the magician makes a person disappear and reappear correctly.

If one-tenth of the time the audience member doesn't reappear, and 100 performances have been done this year, that means one-tenth of 100, or 10, times the person does not reappear. The people that disappear are a separate group from the people that reappear, so 10 people can disappear and not reappear.

To find the correct way to reappear, we need to calculate the number of times that was not a case of 10 people disappearing. This means, get rid of 10 and divide 100 by 10 (since each time the person does not reappear, one more person can reappear) to find out how many times two people must reappear. One-fifth of 100 is 20 times two people. Since two people reappear as a group of two, there must be one time where two people do not reappear and one time where one person does not reappear. \boxed{20} is the number of people that reappear as two.

So we find one of three reappears where correct reappears are 20; ones where two reappears are 20; ones where two does not reappear are 10.

